<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qazi Muhammad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">Qazi</a> Muhammad was a Kurdish judge and religious leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a> who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> and became president of the Soviet-backed Republic of Mahabad, proclaimed in January 1946. When Soviet troops left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Iranian forces reoccupied Mahabad, and he was hanged in March 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qazi-muhammad.jpg" alt="Qazi Muhammad" width="266" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown yet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qazi_Muhammad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Qazi Muhammad was born on 1 May 1893 into a prominent land-owning Sunni Kurdish family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a>, the son of Qazi Ali, a highly respected judge. His mother came from the Fayzullahbegi tribe, though he never claimed allegiance to it. He was educated at a religious school and by his father, and spoke Kurdish, Persian, Turkish, Arabic and a few Western languages. He headed the Religious Endowment Department of Mahabad before succeeding his father as judge, and was accepted as a nationalist leader as well as a religious figure. Since the Soviet occupation of 1941, Mahabad had been effectively under him and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadr">Sadr</a>-i Qazi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1942 a group of 18 intellectuals founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-for-the-revival-of-kurdistan">Society for the Revival of Kurdistan</a>. Qazi Muhammad never formally joined its central committee but was invited in October 1944 to be its spiritual leader. In September 1945 he headed a Kurdish delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> to meet Mir Jafar Baghirov, who told them they needed a disciplined party with its own army if they wanted Soviet support. The society was closed, and Qazi Muhammad established the Kurdistan Democratic Party in 1945. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-barzani">Mustafa Barzani</a> crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> with thousands of fighters on 11 October 1945 and became its backbone, though Qazi disagreed with him on many points.</p><p>On 17 December 1945 the Kurdish flag was raised over the Ministry of Justice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a>. Qazi Muhammad declared the Republic of Mahabad on 22 January 1946, stressing its religious and nationalist nature, and became president on 11 February. Soviet forces completed their withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> by 9 May 1946. The Iranian army then won over many Kurdish and Azeri fighters, most Kurdish tribes sided with it, and by late 1946 Qazi had only around 1,000 tribal fighters. Cut off from exporting tobacco, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> of Mahabad also suffered economic hardship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 13 December 1946 he sent his brother to tell General Homayuni that the party would not resist. The army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a> peacefully on 15 December, and Qazi Muhammad was arrested two days later. A military court-martial tried him, Hussein Saifi Qazi and Sadri Qazi for treason, instigating a revolt and trying to set up an autonomous government under the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. They were hanged at 5:00 a.m. on 31 March 1947.</p><p>He became a martyr among Kurdish nationalists, his grave became a shrine, and 22 January, the day the republic was declared, is observed as a holiday. The Iranian government condemned him as a separatist. His association with the Soviets, whose wartime record in the region was remembered, was his main political liability.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qazi Muhammad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Soviet occupation of northern Iran, Kurdish nationalists in Mahabad formed the Komala society and then the Kurdistan Democratic Party under <b>Qazi Muhammad</b>, a respected judge and religious leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mahabad">Republic of Mahabad</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.77,45.72&amp;year=1937" aria-label="Mahabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5698 839 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Qazi Muhammad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6148 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6148 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.77,45.72&amp;year=1937">What was here in 1937? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
